\section{Introduction}\label{sec:instruction-scheduling-intro}

\emph{Instruction scheduling} reorders instructions to maximize processor utilization and minimize execution time. Modern processors can execute multiple instructions per cycle, but only if the instructions are independent and the required functional units are available. Instruction scheduling exploits this parallelism by reordering instructions to fill delay slots, hide latencies, and keep functional units busy.

This chapter covers the major instruction scheduling techniques: list scheduling, dependence graphs, resource-constrained scheduling, modulo scheduling for loops, and the interaction with VLIW and EPIC architectures.

\section{List Scheduling}\label{sec:list-scheduling}

\emph{List scheduling} is the most widely used instruction scheduling algorithm. It is a greedy heuristic that schedules one instruction at a time, choosing at each step the instruction with the highest priority.

\subsection{Basic Algorithm}\label{sec:list-sched-basic}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{List Scheduling (Basic)}
\label{alg:list-sched}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Dependence DAG $G = (V, E)$, latency function $\mathit{lat}$
\Ensure Scheduled instruction sequence
\State $\mathit{ready} \gets \{v \in V \mid \mathit{pred}(v) = \emptyset\}$ (instructions with no predecessors)
\State $t \gets 0$ (current cycle)
\While{$V \neq \emptyset$}
  \State Select $v \in \mathit{ready}$ with highest priority
  \State Schedule $v$ at time $t$
  \State Remove $v$ from $V$
  \ForAll{$s \in \mathit{succ}(v)$}
    \If{$\mathit{pred}(s) \cap V = \emptyset$}
      \State Add $s$ to $\mathit{ready}$ at time $t + \mathit{lat}(v, s)$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State $t \gets t + 1$
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Priority Functions}\label{sec:priority-functions}

The quality of list scheduling depends critically on the priority function. Common choices include:

\begin{definition}[Priority Functions]\label{def:priority-fns}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Height}: The length of the longest path from the instruction to the exit. Higher is more urgent.
  \item \textbf{Depth}: The length of the longest path from the entry to the instruction. Higher means later start, so schedule earlier.
  \item \textbf{Number of successors}: Instructions with more successors are more urgent (they unblock more work).
  \item \textbf{Resource demand}: Instructions that use rare functional units are scheduled first.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{List Scheduling Directions}\label{sec:list-directions}

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Forward scheduling}: Schedule from entry to exit. Good for minimizing the total schedule length.
  \item \textbf{Backward scheduling}: Schedule from exit to entry. Good for exposing opportunities for forward scheduling.
  \item \textbf{Bidirectional scheduling}: Alternate between forward and backward to find the best schedule.
\end{itemize}

\section{Dependence Graphs}\label{sec:dependence-graphs}

\subsection{Types of Dependences}\label{sec:dependence-types}

\begin{definition}[Dependence Types]\label{def:dep-types}
A dependence between instructions $i$ and $j$ ($i$ before $j$) can be:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{True dependence} (RAW): $i$ produces a value consumed by $j$.
  \item \textbf{Anti-dependence} (WAR): $i$ reads a value later written by $j$.
  \item \textbf{Output dependence} (WAW): $i$ and $j$ write the same location.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Dependence Graph]\label{def:dep-graph}
The \textbf{dependence graph} is a DAG where nodes are instructions and edges represent dependences. Each edge is labeled with the \emph{latency}: the minimum number of cycles that must elapse between the issue of the source and the issue of the destination.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Dependence Analysis}\label{sec:dep-analysis}

Dependences are determined by:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Data dependence analysis}: Determines which memory accesses may alias.
  \item \textbf{Register dependence}: Two instructions accessing the same register have a dependence.
  \item \textbf{Control dependence}: Instructions in the same basic block are ordered by program order.
\end{itemize}

\section{Resource-Constrained Scheduling}\label{sec:resource-constrained}

In practice, instructions compete for limited functional units (ALUs, multipliers, load/store units). \emph{Resource-constrained scheduling} accounts for these limitations.

\subsection{Resource Model}\label{sec:resource-model}

\begin{definition}[Resource Model]\label{def:resource-model}
A \textbf{resource model} specifies:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The set of functional units: $\{U_1, U_2, \ldots, U_m\}$.
  \item The capacity of each unit: $c(U_i)$ instructions per cycle.
  \item The assignment of instructions to units: which instructions can use which units.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Resource Conflict]\label{def:resource-conflict}
A \textbf{resource conflict} occurs when two instructions scheduled in the same cycle require the same functional unit and the unit's capacity is exceeded.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Resource-Constrained List Scheduling}\label{sec:rc-list-sched}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Resource-Constrained List Scheduling}
\label{alg:rc-list-sched}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Dependence DAG, resource model, latency function
\Ensure Scheduled instruction sequence
\State Initialize resource availability for each cycle
\State $\mathit{ready} \gets$ instructions with no predecessors
\State $t \gets 0$
\While{$V \neq \emptyset$}
  \State $\mathit{candidates} \gets \{v \in \mathit{ready} \mid \text{resources available at time } t\}$
  \If{$\mathit{candidates} \neq \emptyset$}
    \State Select $v \in \mathit{candidates}$ with highest priority
    \State Schedule $v$ at time $t$, reserve resources
  \Else
    \State $t \gets t + 1$ (no instruction can be scheduled)
  \EndIf
  \State Update $\mathit{ready}$ set
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Modulo Scheduling for Loops}\label{sec:modulo-scheduling}

\emph{Modulo scheduling} (see Section~\ref{sec:modulo-scheduling}) is the primary technique for scheduling loop bodies. It overlaps instructions from different iterations to maximize throughput.

\subsection{Initiation Interval}\label{sec:ii-detail}

\begin{definition}[Minimum Initiation Interval]\label{def:mii}
The \textbf{minimum initiation interval} (MII) is the lower bound on the II:
\[
\mathit{MII} = \max(\mathit{RecMII}, \mathit{ResMII})
\]
where:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\mathit{RecMII}$: The recurrence MII, determined by the longest dependence cycle.
  \item $\mathit{ResMII}$: The resource MII, determined by the functional unit utilization.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\subsection{The Modulo Scheduling Algorithm}\label{sec:modulo-algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Modulo Scheduling Algorithm}
\label{alg:modulo-sched}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Loop body, dependence graph, resource model
\Ensure Modulo schedule with II
\State Compute $\mathit{MII}$
\State $\mathit{II} \gets \mathit{MII}$
\Loop
  \State Attempt to schedule all instructions with initiation interval $\mathit{II}$
  \If{scheduling succeeds}
    \State \Return schedule with II
  \Else
    \State $\mathit{II} \gets \mathit{II} + 1$
    \If{$\mathit{II} > \mathit{limit}$}
      \State \Return failure (use unrolled loop)
    \EndIf
  \EndIf
\EndLoop
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{VLIW and EPIC Architectures}\label{sec:vliw-epic}

\subsection{VLIW}\label{sec:vliw}

\emph{Very Long Instruction Word} (VLIW) architectures expose the instruction scheduling problem to the compiler. Each VLIW instruction contains multiple operations that execute in parallel.

\begin{definition}[VLIW Instruction]\label{def:vliw}
A \textbf{VLIW instruction} is a fixed-width word containing multiple \emph{operations}, one for each functional unit. The hardware executes all operations in the instruction simultaneously, with no dynamic scheduling.
\end{definition}

\subsection{EPIC}\label{sec:epic}

\emph{Explicitly Parallel Instruction Computing} (EPIC), as used in the Intel Itanium architecture, extends VLIW with compiler-provided hints about parallelism:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Instruction groups}: Groups of instructions that can execute in parallel.
  \item \textbf{Stop bits}: Markers indicating that all previous instructions must complete before the next group starts.
  \item \textbf{Predication}: Conditional execution without branches.
\end{itemize}

\section{Software Pipelining}\label{sec:sw-pipelining-sched}

(See Section~\ref{sec:software-pipelining} for the detailed treatment.) Software pipelining is the application of modulo scheduling to generate pipelined loop code. The key concepts are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Initiation interval}: The number of cycles between the start of successive iterations.
  \item \emph{Prologue}: The ramp-up phase where the pipeline fills.
  \item \emph{Steady state}: The pipelined loop body.
  \item \emph{Epilogue}: The drain phase where the pipeline empties.
\end{itemize}

\begin{historical}
Instruction scheduling has evolved alongside processor architectures. Early compilers performed no scheduling, relying on the hardware to handle instruction ordering. The development of VLIW architectures in the 1980s (Fisher~\cite{fisher1983very}, 1983) made instruction scheduling a compiler responsibility. List scheduling was developed by Landman and Schwartz~\cite{landman1970dirty} (1970) and refined by many others. Modulo scheduling was developed by Lam~\cite{lam1988software} (1988) and Huff~\cite{huff1993lifetime} (1993). Modern superscalar processors perform dynamic scheduling in hardware, but static scheduling by the compiler remains important for VLIW, embedded processors, and loop optimization.
\end{historical}

\section{Scheduling with Speculation}\label{sec:speculative-scheduling}

\emph{Speculative scheduling} moves instructions before their dependences are resolved, betting that the dependences will not cause conflicts.

\begin{definition}[Speculative Scheduling]\label{def:speculative-sched}
\textbf{Speculative scheduling} moves an instruction before a potentially conflicting instruction, with a check to verify that the speculation was correct. If the check fails, the instruction is re-executed.
\end{definition}

Types of speculation:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Speculative load}: Move a load before a potentially aliasing store.
  \item \textbf{Speculative check}: Insert a check after the store to verify the load got the correct value.
\end{itemize}

\section{Scheduling for Out-of-Order Processors}\label{sec:ooo-scheduling}

Modern out-of-order processors perform dynamic scheduling in hardware. However, static scheduling by the compiler can still help:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Register pressure reduction}: Fewer registers means less renaming hardware needed.
  \item \textbf{Branch hint instructions}: Help the processor's branch predictor.
  \item \textbf{Prefetch instructions}: Explicitly load data into cache before it is needed.
  \item \textbf{NOP insertion}: Align instructions to decode boundaries.
\end{itemize}

