\section{Reserving Future Pipeline Stages}

Checking only the issue slot can miss a conflict several cycles later. Two different instructions can start on separate execution units but require the same writeback port at the same future time. A list scheduler can model this by keeping a reservation table over resource and cycle pairs.

\textbf{Inputs and assumptions.} Each machine instruction has a finite set of legal alternatives. An alternative lists resource demands $(r,\delta,q)$: $q$ units of resource $r$ at offset $\delta\geq 0$ after issue. Include an issue-width resource, and combine duplicate demands at the same resource and offset. The caller has already checked dependency readiness at the tentative issue time, using latencies appropriate for the alternative if they differ.

\begin{sudocode}[Reserve a legal pipeline alternative]
\Require Ready instruction $i$, issue cycle $t$, table $R$
\Ensure Reserve one feasible alternative or leave $R$ unchanged
\ForAll{legal alternatives $a$ of $i$}
  \State $ok\gets\text{true}$
  \ForAll{$(r,\delta,q)\in a$}
    \If{$R[r,t+\delta]+q>\mathit{cap}(r)$}
      \State $ok\gets\text{false}$ \Comment{Check future cycles too}
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \If{$ok$}
    \ForAll{$(r,\delta,q)\in a$}
      \State $R[r,t+\delta]\gets R[r,t+\delta]+q$
    \EndFor
    \State \Return $a$ \Comment{Commit only after every check}
  \EndIf
\EndFor
\State \Return no fit
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Example.} Suppose there is one writeback port and the issue width is two. An instruction issued at cycle 0 writes back at offset 3. An independent instruction considered at cycle 1 writes back at offset 2. Although cycle 1 has a free issue slot, both would use the port at cycle 3. Moving the second instruction to cycle 2 puts its writeback at cycle 4 and fits.

The two reservation strips in \Cref{fig:visual-79} distinguish an
open issue slot from an available writeback slot.

