\textbf{Invariant.} Every committed slot stays within capacity; a failed trial cannot reserve only some of its slots. Testing alternatives takes time proportional to their total number of demand entries. This check establishes legality for the model, not global optimality: the first fitting alternative may block a better schedule later. An alternative whose single-instruction demand exceeds capacity must be rejected at every issue time.

\section{Summary}\label{sec:instruction-scheduling-summary}

Instruction scheduling reorders instructions to maximize processor utilization. List scheduling is a greedy heuristic that selects the highest-priority ready instruction at each step. Dependence graphs capture the constraints on reordering. Resource-constrained scheduling accounts for limited functional units. Modulo scheduling overlaps loop iterations for maximum throughput. VLIW and EPIC architectures require the compiler to perform all scheduling statically. Speculative scheduling moves instructions before dependences are resolved. Even for out-of-order processors, static scheduling helps with register pressure and instruction alignment. The quality of instruction scheduling directly affects the performance of generated code, particularly for loops and compute-intensive kernels.

\section{Modulo Scheduling in Detail}\label{sec:modulo-scheduling-detail}

\emph{Modulo scheduling} is the most aggressive form of software pipelining, overlapping instructions from different iterations to maximize throughput.

\begin{definition}[Resource-Constrained MII]\label{def:res-mii}
The \textbf{resource-constrained minimum initiation interval} (ResMII) is:
\[
\text{ResMII} = \max_{r \in resources} \left\lceil \frac{usage(r)}{capacity(r)} \right\rceil
\]
where $usage(r)$ is the number of units of resource $r$ needed per iteration, and $capacity(r)$ is the available amount.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Recurrence-Constrained MII]\label{def:rec-mii}
The \textbf{recurrence-constrained minimum initiation interval} (RecMII) is:
\[
\text{RecMII} = \max_{cycles\ C} \left\lceil \frac{\sum_{e \in C} latency(e)}{iterations(C)} \right\rceil
\]
where the maximum is over all cycles $C$ in the dependence graph, and $iterations(C)$ is the number of iterations spanned by the cycle.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[RecMII Calculation]\label{ex:recmii}
\begin{verbatim}
  // Loop with floating-point recurrence:
  for (i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    a: r1 = load A[i]       // latency: 4 cycles
    b: r2 = fmul r1, r3     // latency: 3 cycles, depends on a
    c: r3 = fadd r2, r4     // latency: 2 cycles, depends on b
    d: store r3, B[i]       // depends on c
  }
  
  // Recurrence: b → c → b (r3 is loop-carried)
  // Cycle latency: 3 + 2 = 5 cycles
  // Iterations spanned: 1
  // RecMII = ⌈5/1⌉ = 5
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\subsection{Priority Heuristics for Modulo Scheduling}\label{sec:modulo-priority}

The order in which instructions are scheduled affects the quality of the result:

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\hline
\textbf{Heuristic} & \textbf{Description} \\
\hline
Height & Schedule instructions with longest path to sink first \\
Depth & Schedule instructions with longest path from source first \\
Successors count & Schedule instructions with most successors first \\
Critical path & Schedule instructions on the critical path first \\
Resource pressure & Schedule instructions using scarce resources first \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\caption{Priority heuristics for modulo scheduling.}
\label{tab:modulo-priority}
\end{table}

\subsection{Register Pressure in Modulo Scheduling}\label{sec:modulo-reg-pressure}

\begin{definition}[Modulo Register Pressure]\label{def:modulo-reg-pressure}
In modulo scheduling, a value produced in iteration $i$ and consumed in iteration $i + \delta$ must be kept alive for $\delta \times II + \text{latency}$ cycles. The \textbf{modulo register pressure} is the maximum number of simultaneously live values at any point in the steady state.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Register Pressure Bound]\label{thm:reg-pressure-bound}
The modulo register pressure is bounded by:
\[
RP \leq \sum_{e \in E} \left\lceil \frac{distance(e)}{II} \right\rceil
\]
where $distance(e)$ is the dependence distance of edge $e$. If $RP$ exceeds the available registers, the II must be increased or values must be spilled.
\end{theorem}

\section{VLIW and EPIC Scheduling}\label{sec:vliw-epic}

\emph{Very Long Instruction Word} (VLIW) and \emph{Explicitly Parallel Instruction Computing} (EPIC) architectures require the compiler to explicitly encode parallelism.

\begin{definition}[VLIW Instruction Format]\label{def:vliw-format}
A \textbf{VLIW instruction} is a wide word containing multiple operations, one for each functional unit:
\begin{verbatim}
  | ALU1 op | ALU2 op | MEM op | BRANCH op |
  |  20 bits |  20 bits | 20 bits |  10 bits  |
\end{verbatim}
All operations in a VLIW instruction execute in parallel. The compiler must ensure no conflicts.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[EPIC Extensions]\label{def:epic}
\textbf{EPIC} (used in Intel Itanium) extends VLIW with:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Predicate registers}: Conditional execution without branches
  \item \textbf{Dependency hints}: Compiler-provided dependence information
  \item \textbf{Software pipelining support}: Hardware loop buffers
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{VLIW List Scheduling}
\label{alg:vliw-scheduling}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Dependence DAG $D$, functional units $FU_1, \ldots, FU_k$, target II
\ensure Scheduled instruction sequence
\State $cycle \gets 0$
\While{unscheduled instructions remain}
  \State Find ready set: instructions with all predecessors scheduled
  \For{each functional unit $FU_j$ at time $cycle$}
    \If{ready set has instruction $i$ compatible with $FU_j$}
      \State Select highest-priority instruction $i$
      \State Schedule $i$ on $FU_j$ at time $cycle$
      \State Remove $i$ from ready set
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State $cycle \gets cycle + 1$
\EndWhile
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Trace Scheduling}\label{sec:trace-scheduling}

\begin{definition}[Trace Scheduling]\label{def:trace-scheduling}
\textbf{Trace scheduling} schedules instructions along the most likely execution path (the \emph{trace}), optimizing for the common case. Instructions that may execute on side entrances or exits are compensated with fixup code.
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Trace Scheduling}
\label{alg:trace-scheduling}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require CFG with profile data, selected trace $T$
\ensure Scheduled code for $T$ with compensation
\State Extract trace $T$ as a straight-line sequence of blocks
\State Schedule $T$ using list scheduling (ignoring side entrances/exits)
\ForAll{side entrances to $T$}
  \State Generate compensation code to restore state
\EndFor
\ForAll{side exits from $T$}
  \State Generate compensation code to fix state for exit targets
\EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\begin{example}[Trace Scheduling]\label{ex:trace-scheduling}
\begin{verbatim}
  // Trace: A → B → D (most likely path, probability 80%)
  // Side entrance: C → B (probability 20%)
  // Side exit: B → E (probability 10%)
  
  // Scheduled trace (optimized for A → B → D):
  // Instructions from A, B, D interleaved for maximum ILP
  
  // Compensation at C → B entrance:
  // Save/restore registers that trace scheduling assumed were free
  
  // Compensation at B → E exit:
  // Restore values that trace scheduling moved past their original position
\end{verbatim}
\end{example}

\section{Scheduling for Superscalar Processors}\label{sec:superscalar-scheduling}

\begin{definition}[Issue Width]\label{def:issue-width}
The \textbf{issue width} is the maximum number of instructions the processor can dispatch per cycle. Superscalar processors have issue width 2--8; the compiler must find enough independent instructions to fill each cycle.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[NOP Insertion for Alignment]\label{def:nop-insertion}
\textbf{NOP insertion} adds no-operation instructions to align critical code to decode boundaries, branch target buffers, or cache lines. This prevents instruction fetch stalls.
\end{definition}

